     "textwithimage": {
      "jcr:primaryType": "nt:unstructured",
      "fileReference": "/content/dam/macnicaglobal2/americas/mai/en/products/boards-modules/tq-systems/arm-modules/TQMa62xx Embedded Cortex®-A53 module.jpeg",
      "imageRatio": "40",
      "jcr:lastModifiedBy": "bruno.jaques@macnicadhw.com.br",
      "alt": "TQMa62xx Embedded Cortex®-A53 module",
      "text": "<h1>Based on the TI AM62xx for applications with enhanced computing &amp; scalable graphics performance</h1>\n<p>The TQMa62xx embedded module, based on the Texas Instruments AM62xx processor family, offers advanced computing and scalable graphics performance for a wide range of applications. With options for Quad, Dual, or Single Cortex-A53 cores, this module provides scalable computing power to meet varying performance requirements.</p>\n<p>One standout feature is its dual-display capability, supporting LVDS and RGB interfaces, complemented by a 3D GPU for enhanced graphics performance. The module supports LPDDR4 memory with ECC, ensuring data integrity in demanding applications.</p>\n<p>For high-speed communication, the TQMa62xx features 2x Gbit Ethernet switches with TSN support, along with 3x CAN FD interfaces and 2x USB 2.0 ports. Despite its robust performance, this module maintains low power consumption, typically operating at just 2W.</p>\n<p>Security is a priority, as the module integrates security functions for data protection. The TQMa62xx is backed by Texas Instruments, a trusted partner in embedded solutions, and is designed to accommodate pin-compatible processors within a single module design.</p>\n<p>Whether in industrial automation, robotics, or other demanding applications, the TQMa62xx empowers engineers with the computing and graphics capabilities needed to drive innovation and efficiency.</p>",
      "displayPopupTitle": "true",
      "linkTarget": "_self",
      "jcr:lastModified": "Tue Sep 01 2026 01:20:00 GMT+0000",
      "titleValueFromDAM": "true",
      "sling:resourceType": "macnicaglobal2/components/content/textwithimage",
